Chapter 10: The Land of the Lion King

Chapter 10.38: “Wait”

The circumstances surrounding Kugelschreiber Castle had suddenly changed.

Hearing the news conveyed by Tiga, Subaru and the others couldn't help but hold their breath.

The castle had traversed space. In other words, it had escaped from the hidden alternate dimension and returned to the outside world for the first time in several hundred years.

However, that meant—

“Looks like this was unexpected even for you.”

“—. Well, who knows? Maybe it's all part of our plan to make you think that? Trusting too much in what you see with your own eyes is one of your bad habits, my friend. I've been telling you that for years, haven't I?”

“Lord Subaru, the wind says he's lying…”

“Shhh! Miss Crusch, shhh! We're in the middle of an advanced battle of wits!”

“That's not happening. You're not fooling anyone. …Though I will admit, being told there's something I've forgotten does make my heart skip a beat a little.”

After checking Subaru and the others' reactions, Tiga glanced toward Subaru's side, where Melty stood, still watching him.

Melty's gaze as she stared at Tiga was filled with expectation and burning emotion, carrying a significance that was difficult to ignore. The fact that she'd called his name made it clear that there was some kind of relationship between them.

“And it seems like the same goes for you, too… Black Hound, what's the story with your past and that little spirit?”

“I don't remember a single thing! Not one bit! Not even the tiniest little scrap! It's the fault of that teeny-tiny spirit I've never met before! Sorry about that! I'm so sorry!”

“…Oh, is that so? Don't worry about it.”

Like Melty, the Black Hound had some complicated history of his own. Apparently, he also had an unusual relationship with the squirrel-like spirit perched on Tiga's shoulder, glaring at them.

The Black Hound was acting like this, but based on everything Subaru had experienced over the past two months, he judged that the spirit probably wasn't lying.

To put it bluntly, the sheer volume of information being crammed into his head was making him dizzy. But now that Belalis—the source of much of that dizziness—had fallen silent, he couldn't afford to use information overload as an excuse to stop thinking.

—At present, aside from the lively Black Hound and a somewhat lively Crusch, their side consisted of Subaru, Ferris, and Melty, all of whom were half-dead.

Even the first two were far from being in fighting condition, thanks to their injuries and exhaustion. They absolutely couldn't afford to do anything reckless.

Still, they did have a glimmer of hope.

And to bring that hope to fruition—

“Now then, we've got five people on our side, while you've only got two, even counting the spirit on your shoulder. Numerically speaking, we have more than twice your numbers, giving us an overwhelming advantage. If you want to surrender, now's your chance!”

“…Am I hearing things? That guy's completely ignoring how battered they are and talking to us like that just because he's counting heads.”

“No, you're hearing him correctly, Pooka. That's just how my friend is. Though I have to admit, even I'm surprised he's completely disregarding how exhausted everyone is.”

“You have more seriously injured people on your side. That's another advantage for us.”

“When you say that so confidently with a hole in your back, I almost feel like believing you.”

Tiga shrugged as though he had absolutely no intention of believing Subaru. Apparently, he had no talent for acting whatsoever.

For now, Subaru had learned the squirrel-like spirit's name was Pooka. He'd also discovered that their opponents were willing to entertain a conversation, which was something.

Nevertheless, as their exchange dragged on as little more than casual conversation rather than anything resembling negotiations, Crusch and the Black Hound gradually grew more eager to fight, naturally sensing that a battle was unavoidable—

“—Hey! What the hell are you taking your sweet time talking for?! Get on with it! Do it! Kill them already, Executor! That's what you're here for, isn't it?!”

However, the one who grew impatient and raised his voice even more than Subaru's group was Reborn.

Using his one-sided tailor shears for support, Reborn had lost his eyesight to Crusch's sword strikes and could no longer use his vision-dependent power, Silence. Even so, relying on his sense of presence, he shouted at Tiga, demanding that he fulfill his duty by which he'd come here.

Hearing the plea, Subaru moistened his dry lips with his tongue, thinking that Reborn really didn't need to say that.

“…Honestly, being attacked without any warning would be the worst possible outcome.”

Even if the restrictions imposed by Silence and Ring Finger had disappeared, allowing Subaru's mouth and mind to work as they normally did, that alone wouldn't be enough to overcome the decisive difference in strength.

He could help support their combat capabilities through the cheat-like combination of Cor Leonis and Blue, but there was no guarantee how long that combination would last.

That was why the most troublesome thing Tiga could do right now was force his way through with sheer power—

“Now, hurry up and take these guys—”

“—Torner, you can hear me, right? Use Detour at full power. We're withdrawing from here.”

“Wha—?!”

However, Tiga's next words betrayed both Subaru's fears and Reborn's demands.

Calling out by name to the third Beloved Child, who wasn't present, Tiga spun around on the spot and closed the distance to Reborn, whose bloodied face was turned toward him.

Then, using the pommel of the curved sword he'd drawn, he delivered a powerful blow to the back of the standing Reborn's neck.

“Guh—”

“I told you. Time's up. Besides, you guys are the ones at fault for rushing ahead without waiting for instructions.”

The small-framed man let out a brief groan before collapsing. Casually hoisting him over his shoulder, Tiga spoke dismissively.

Then he turned toward the stunned Subaru and the others.

“You heard me. We're taking our spoiled brats and withdrawing. That's all for today.”

“Hey, hey, hey! That's a pretty damn selfish opinion you've got there, isn't it?! You barge in here out of nowhere, throw out a bunch of riddles, and then just walk away?! Leave your lives and your answers behind!”

“W-what vulgar and barbaric language! I can't believe someone like that is my contractor! Have I been deceived?!”

“How the hell should I know, you idiot?! Take your baggage and burn out in a blaze of glory! BURNING!”

With those shouted words serving as the grand finale to their argument, the Black Hound thrust out his palm and unleashed a raging inferno.

The green flames spread horizontally like the breath of a dragon, cutting off every avenue of escape for Tiga, who had the indignant spirit perched on his shoulder.

Faced with this completely unexpected act of recklessness, Subaru nearly let out a scream of his own.

But an even louder voice rang out from beside him, coming from Melty.

It wasn't a scream, however.

“Don't! Fire won't work on Pooka!”

“Huh?!”

The Black Hound responded with a puzzled shout at her confident declaration.

The truth of her words was proven when the green flames reached Tiga and his companions—

And were devoured head-on.

At the center of the approaching green inferno, the section directly in front of Tiga and the others was neatly bitten away and disappeared.

The culprit was Pooka, who licked her lips with relish.

The adorable-looking spirit lightly wiped the corner of her mouth with one forepaw.

“That girl seems to know quite a lot. —That I'm a Great Spirit of Fire.”

“If you knew that, then say something! Are you trying to show off how evil you are here?! You're certainly devoted to your own self-promotion!”

“The Black Hound attacked out of nowhere! More importantly—”

After retorting to the Black Hound's unjustified complaint, Melty shifted her gaze toward Tiga on the other side of the divided flames.

As though responding to that signal, Tiga lightly leaped backward while carrying Reborn. Then, extending one arm, he scooped up the fallen Belalis from the ground.

“You're still alive, right? Spread Ring Finger around. No need to hold back. —It won't work on me anyway.”

“…Oh? Does that mean… you trust me, and you love me? I love you, too…!”

Just how tenacious was she?

At Tiga's words, Belalis responded with affection without having learned a thing. Then came that sweet fragrance, which heightened emotions and robbed people of their sanity.

Belalis's Ring Finger activated, and once again, the substance that caused emotions to spiral out of control began spreading through the air.

“Ferris! I'm gonna be in trouble, so please deal with this for me! Yahoo!”

“—! You're already in trouble! Don't move, don't thrash around! I can't treat you like this!”

Using Cor Leonis, Subaru activated their combination technique: taking on the mental abnormalities affecting all his companions and having Ferris treat him for them.

Somehow, they managed to hold on.

But even as they dealt with the situation, Tiga—carrying Belalis over his shoulder like a sack of rice—was making his escape from the battlefield with the two defeated Beloved Children in his arms.

Crusch and the Black Hound each moved to stop him, but—

“I won't let you interfere!”

“—!”

Pooka puffed out her cheeks and roared, spitting back the flames she'd just swallowed from the Black Hound.

The green inferno swept toward the two who had been trying to pursue Tiga.

Crusch severed the flames with an upward slash of her sword and stepped through the curtain of fire—

Only to collide head-on with the Black Hound.

“Kyah?!”

“Whoa?!”

Their startled cries overlapped as both of them toppled over from the unexpected collision.

It was a repeat of the friendly fire caused by Ring Finger—

Or rather, it wasn't.

“—WHY?! Tiga, where'd you go?!”

On the other side of the stumbling Crusch and the Black Hound, Subaru had also lost sight of Tiga.

It had happened immediately after Pooka's flames briefly obscured his vision. The instant the flames blocked his view, Tiga and his companions had vanished.

Having lost sight of someone who should have been right there, Subaru doubted his own eyes.

But he wasn't the only one who'd lost track of Tiga. Ferris, who was leaning against Subaru with his arms missing, also stared around in confusion, his eyes darting back and forth.

“Huh? Huh?”

Subaru could feel Ferris's healing power more closely than ever.

In other words, this wasn't the effect of Belalis's Ring Finger—

“—Detour?! I thought it only made people get lost?!”

Detour had been responsible for separating their team inside the castle and making it difficult for them to reunite afterward.

If the ability, whose user had yet to reveal themselves, could distort not only one's sense of direction but also one's ability to perceive things, then it had diverted Subaru's gaze and attention, causing him to lose sight of Tiga.

And just like that, they would be forced to let Tiga escape, running outside their awareness with no way to stop him—

“Wait!”

At that moment, Melty raised her thin voice and stepped forward, blocking the way with her casting staff.

Subaru had barely caught sight of her at the edge of his vision. Forcing his head to turn in her direction and resisting Detour, he saw Melty standing in front of the door leading out of the underground, facing the approaching Tiga.

Tiga's gaze sharpened as he approached, carrying two Beloved Children with both arms occupied.

“Get out of the way! You'll get hurt!”

“Then you'll have to take responsibility!”

“What kind of answer is that?!”

Tiga's eyes widened in astonishment at Melty's response as she blocked his path.

Their distance continued to shrink. It seemed that Melty was about to pay the price for her recklessness—

“Damn it! Pooka!”

“Hold on to those guys tightly!”

At Tiga's call, Pooka leaped from his shoulder and rapidly grew in size.

Her original twenty-centimeter body swelled before their eyes until she was a gigantic creature approximately five meters tall.

Her round tail grew proportionally larger, then swung around and gently struck Tiga in the back.

The impact sent him flying forward.

“—Tiga!”

Tiga sailed through the air in a parabola, passing over Melty's head as she stared up at him with wide, round eyes.

She reflexively reached out, but the height and momentum he'd gained put him beyond her reach.

“See you.”

“Tiga—!!”

Their eyes met for just an instant.

Then, from the very first step after landing, Tiga shot forward like an arrow, leaving Melty behind as she desperately screamed after him.

His feet didn't stop.

And Detour ensured that she couldn't catch up to his retreating figure.

“Wait! Wait, I said! Tiga! Tigaaa!”

No matter how many times she shouted, it was as though her voice couldn't reach him.

Her outstretched hand couldn't reach him either.

The distance between them continued to grow, and eventually, even the sound of his footsteps disappeared.

Behind him, Pooka's gigantic form also dissolved into particles of mana and vanished, following the departing Tiga.

“Damn it, he got away with it…”

They'd let him escape.

—That was the conclusion they had to accept as the result of their encounter with the Beloved Children at Kugelschreiber Castle, a battle whose outcome had been left frustratingly unresolved because they hadn't managed to finish what they'd started.

　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　△▼△▼△▼△

—With the unexpected arrival of Tiga Laureon and Pooka, the battle's conclusion had been thoroughly muddled.

Honestly, all Subaru could say was that Tiga had completely outplayed them.

Regardless of whether the information they'd obtained was trustworthy, if they'd managed to secure the two defeated Beloved Children, they might have been able to get at least a little closer to uncovering the secrets of the mysterious Beloved Children.

Even if that hadn't worked, they could at least have reduced the enemy's fighting strength.

But all of that had gone up in smoke because their captives had been taken back.

“Still, if the fight had continued, we would've been the ones in trouble, huh? I never expected Tiga to be a spirit arts user. He really had us fooled.”

“—No, that's not it. Tiga isn't a spirit arts user. He should simply be someone who's together with Pooka…”

“You mean without a contract? Like Anastasia and Eridona? …If that's the case, then what exactly is the relationship between those two?”

“That's… I'm sorry. I don't know. All I know is that they aren't in a contractual relationship.”

Clutching her now-shortened casting staff, Melty lowered her head and gave an ambiguous answer.

But Subaru didn't think her uncertainty came from lying or trying to evade the question.

Most likely, Melty genuinely didn't know much about the relationship between Tiga and Pooka.

However, things should have been different when it came to her own relationship with Tiga.

“—I'll definitely tell you about it later. But right now…”

“I know. Checking the situation inside Kugelschreiber Castle and reuniting with Beako and the others takes priority… Ferris! What about your arms?! Can you actually reattach them?!”

“I'm concentrating right now, so stop talking to me! I'm suppressing the pain, and it's making my hands shake!”

“Your hands are shaking? Your hands were cut off, so you don't even have hands to shake…”

Ferris snapped back in his high-pitched voice.

He was in the middle of using healing magic to reattach his arms, which he'd retrieved from where they'd been pinned to the wall—the portions from his elbows down that had been severed.

Even Subaru hadn't anticipated that Ferris's arms would actually be cut off. If they couldn't be restored, Subaru would have to spend the rest of his life making amends for it.

When he'd said something to that effect, Ferris had given him another look of absolute disgust.

“Besides, if anyone has to spend their whole life making amends, I'd say it should be the person who actually cut them off.”

“I feel pretty responsible for making that happen, too. Anyway, Miss Crusch, how are you holding up?”

“I have no serious injuries. I can still—”

She had presumably intended to continue by saying she could still fight.

But contrary to her determination, her body couldn't keep up with her will.

—Suddenly, her knees buckled, and her body began to collapse.

Subaru immediately reached out and hurriedly caught her before she fell.

“Whoa! Careful! Are you okay?”

“Ah… Subaru…?”

“It's probably a side effect of Dragon's Eye. Miss Crusch, you've been pushing yourself incredibly hard.”

“But I can still…”

“No, you can't. Don't push yourself. Get some rest for now. The rest of us will make up the difference. Mainly the Black Hound, though.”

Looking at Crusch as she weakly shifted her body, Subaru answered her firmly.

The Black Hound reacted with an indignant, “Whuuut?” but Subaru silenced him with a glare.

Subaru then took the eyepatch from the pocket of Crusch's jacket and gently placed it over her left eye, whose eyelid was growing heavy.

“We'll talk again when you wake up. About the story of how hard you've been working and everything.”

“—Yes…”

She nodded as her consciousness faded, and the eye not covered by the eyepatch closed as well.

Over the past two months, whenever Crusch had been forced to rely on the power of Dragon's Eye, she'd exhausted herself and fallen asleep. Subaru had always thought that even in sleep, she seemed stern and tense, as though she couldn't afford to let her guard down.

But now, her sleeping face seemed to have lost some of that severity.

That sight brought Subaru a small measure of relief.

“—Subaru-kun.”

Ferris was standing in front of Subaru, who had readjusted his hold on the sleeping Crusch.

His arms extended from the sleeves of his black suit, and he demonstrated their recovery by opening and closing his hands, as if showing them off.

Seeing that they'd been properly reattached was another relief.

“Honestly, it was so gruesome I could barely look at it… But you can heal injuries like that?”

“Yep. As long as the missing body part is still there and the person's od hasn't forgotten what its original state was like, I think I can heal it pretty much perfectly.”

“I feel like you just casually said something absolutely outrageous, but whatever. I'm just glad. …Is it okay if I carry Miss Crusch? I'm the one with the most free hands right now.”

“—Mm. Thanks. I hate you.”

“A tsundere cross-dressing boy is a whole new experience…”

Apparently, Subaru's plan to rely on Ferris had done some serious damage to Ferris's opinion of him.

Considering how low that opinion had probably been to begin with, Subaru was afraid to ask whether it had now plummeted into the negatives.

Even so, they couldn't have driven back the Beloved Children without Ferris—and without Crusch.

There were all sorts of emotional complications, but everyone had done their best and managed to secure a result.

“Even if Tiga ended up ruining it for us at the last minute…!”

“U-um, I'm sorry. I'll apologize on Tiga's behalf…”

“Hah! What's with this goody-two-shoes act, apologizing on his behalf?! As if that could make up for the loneliness and misery! Or maybe it could! Whatever! So, what do we do now?”

“First, stop pestering Melty. After that, just as I said before—we prioritize reuniting with Beako and the others. But we're going to proceed carefully.”

Tiga had escaped with the injured Beloved Children.

The whereabouts of the still-unseen third Beloved Child remained unknown, and it was entirely possible that other enemies were still lying in wait inside the castle.

They knew far too little about this place to let their guard down and assume the enemy had been completely driven off.

Especially now that the castle had emerged into the outside world—high above the clouds over the Lifauss Highway—and attracted everyone's attention.

“After all, they say this castle was made by Echidna. Knowing her, she might come up with some trial where we have to escape before the castle comes crashing down, just to test the challengers who've entered it.”

“What kind of impression do you have of her? She's Beatrice-chan's mother, isn't she?”

“…A twisted personality, just barely falling short of being an Archbishop of Sin?”

Strictly speaking, when it came to the sheer nastiness of her character, she wasn't much different from an Archbishop of Sin.

But since she was already dead and couldn't cause any more damage, Subaru had settled on that assessment.

That said, there was also the matter of Sphinx, who'd wreaked havoc in the Vollachian Empire. Depending on what sins Echidna had concealed, Subaru's evaluation of her could still plummet.

Looking around at his companions, all of whom wore expressions of distaste at his answer, Subaru continued.

“Anyway, pay close attention to the floors and walls. If we've made it out of the alternate dimension, we probably won't end up falling endlessly until we die, but…”

“—Ah, you don't need to worry quite that much.”

At the unexpected voice, Subaru whipped around.

At this point, he really didn't want to deal with yet another unexpected intruder, but his fear and anxiety proved unnecessary.

From the stairwell connecting the underground space to the upper floors, a familiar face appeared—

“—Rachins! You're safe!”

“Don't go casually calling me by my first name. That's what I'd like to say, but… Damn, you guys are completely wrecked. You look even worse than the Librarian told me.”

The person looking down at Subaru and the others gathered in the underground area was Rachins, who'd gone off on his own before their encounter with the Beloved Children.

Unlike Subaru's group, who'd been through one brutal battle after another, Rachins didn't appear to have been caught up in any fighting.

It seemed he'd been lucky enough to avoid encountering any enemies.

But—

“Mr. Russell… What about the Silver Fox? Weren't you two together?”

“Safe, more or less. Apparently, that bastard figured out how to keep us from being spotted by the enemy. We were carrying some documents and heading back toward the entrance when the value of the entrance suddenly plummeted, or something like that…”

“Value? You mean the Divine Protection of Appraisal? Didn't know it could be used like that…”

In other words, the entrance to the castle had suddenly suffered a drastic drop in value.

For Russell, who possessed the Divine Protection of Appraisal, the entrance had apparently gone from something useful to something harmful.

If he'd sensed the abnormality at that point, then it made sense that he'd been able to avoid the Beloved Children.

Honestly, Subaru wished they'd been warned, but since they'd been traveling separately, there wasn't much to be done about it.

For now, he would be grateful that nothing irreversible had happened to them while they were apart.

“We've got a lot to talk about, but Long-Tongue-kun, things are pretty crazy outside, aren't they?”

“Oh, right! Apparently, the castle just flew out of the alternate dimension. It might be a witch's trap to wipe out all the intruders at once. We need to—”

“That's not what's happening. The Librarian is making it fly, and the Silver Fox gave the order.”

“—Beako and Mr. Russell?”

Stopped in the middle of his hurried explanation, Subaru stared at Rachins in surprise.

The unexpected news that Beatrice and Russell were working together was startling enough.

But the fact that they were doing something as spectacular—and utterly lacking in secrecy—as making a castle fly made it even harder to believe.

Based on Subaru's experience working as a collaborator with the Six-Tongued over the past two months, it should have been obvious that Russell hated drawing attention to himself more than anything.

“Those two are safe, right? What were they thinking, flying the castle?! And what were you doing? Why didn't you stop them?”

“Don't bombard me with questions all at once! You're noisy as hell! If you're that curious, why are you standing around chatting with me instead of asking the people themselves?”

“Fair point. Can you use Door Traversal?”

“I can. That's how I got here, too. Damn, what a creepy spell.”

Rachins offered an opinion that would probably make Beatrice puff out her cheeks and stomp her feet if she heard it.

Then he started walking, leading Subaru and the others onward.

After taking a few steps, however, he stopped and looked back over his shoulder.

“Oh, right, right. To answer one of your earlier questions… They're going to turn this castle into a base.”

“Huh?”

“That's the answer to your question about why they'd fly the castle. They're turning it into a base. —If we're going to properly reclaim the kingdom, we can't keep running around from place to place, hiding from everyone forever.”

And so, Rachins explained why Russell, despite his aversion to flashy actions, had nevertheless decided to make the castle fly.
